WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.690
In this video, you back up a SUSE Virtualization virtual machine to an S3 bucket with Veeam Kasten, a partner product, then restore it after it is deleted.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:22.670
Veeam Kasten runs on the cluster. Its Virtual Machines page lists demo-k10, a running SUSE Virtualization virtual machine.

00:00:22.670 --> 00:00:30.530
Kasten protects applications by namespace. The virtual machine has its own namespace, demo-k10, which is unmanaged so far.

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:32.990
Open its options menu,

00:00:32.990 --> 00:00:35.880
and select Create a Policy.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.670
The form fills in the policy name, demo-k10-backup, and the Snapshot action.

00:00:40.670 --> 00:00:47.050
For this demo, set the backup frequency to On Demand. In production, you pick a schedule such as Daily.

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:56.510
Turn on Backups via Snapshot Exports. A snapshot alone stays on the cluster; the export copies it to object storage.

00:00:56.510 --> 00:00:59.900
Open the export location profile list,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:05.870
and choose demo-s3, a location profile for an S3 bucket that was configured earlier.

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:12.330
The demo-k10 namespace is already selected, with all of its resources, including the virtual machine and its disk.

00:01:12.330 --> 00:01:15.250
Select Submit.

00:01:15.250 --> 00:01:19.040
The policy is valid. Open its actions,

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:22.100
and select Run Once.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:31.530
Confirm. Kasten snapshots the disk, then exports the snapshot and the virtual machine definition to the bucket.

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:36.790
In Restore Points, the run created a restore point exported to the demo-s3 profile.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:43.610
Now simulate a loss. In SUSE Virtualization, open the actions of demo-k10,

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:45.970
and select Delete.

00:01:45.970 --> 00:01:51.110
The disk volume is selected too. Select Delete.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:55.390
Back in Kasten, open the actions of the exported restore point,

00:01:55.390 --> 00:01:58.070
and select Restore.

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:05.730
Keep the original namespace, demo-k10. Kasten restores every artifact, including the volume and the virtual machine.

00:02:05.730 --> 00:02:08.200
Select Restore,

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.440
and confirm.

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:18.440
In SUSE Virtualization, demo-k10 is running again, with its disk restored from the bucket.

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:25.423
The virtual machine is back, restored from the exported backup. Next, you use NetApp storage with Trident.
